<section class="reference-section" id="gajendra-moksha"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Gajendra Moksha</h2><a href="/gajendra-moksha.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content">
<p><strong>Srimad Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 8 · chapters 2–4.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the owner-supplied Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi first volume. The book scan is not distributed by this website.</p>
<div class="source-list"><a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Open the supplied first volume</span><small>Internet Archive · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition</small></a><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Bhagavata 8.2–4 chapter sequence</span><small>VedaBase · Sanskrit and English edition</small></a></div>
<ol><li><strong>8.2.1–19</strong><small>Trikuta, Ritumata garden and the lotus lake.</small></li><li><strong>8.2.20–26</strong><small>Gajendra leads the herd into the lake.</small></li><li><strong>8.2.27–33</strong><small>The crocodile’s attack, the long struggle and Gajendra’s decision to seek the Supreme refuge.</small></li><li><strong>8.3.1–29</strong><small>The remembered prayer: the Supreme source, witness, shelter and giver of liberation.</small></li><li><strong>8.3.30–33</strong><small>Vishnu arrives on Garuda, receives the lotus offering and frees Gajendra from the crocodile.</small></li><li><strong>8.4.1–13</strong><small>Huhu’s release, Indradyumna’s previous life and Gajendra’s liberation.</small></li><li><strong>8.4.14–26</strong><small>Concluding praise, blessings and recitation instructions; noted but not reproduced as modern ritual guidance.</small></li></ol>
<p>The extended prayer is summarized rather than translated. The page does not convert the thousand-year struggle into ordinary historical chronology or locate Trikuta on a modern map. “The last reserve was remembrance” and the closing question are editorial reflections.</p>
<p>Four reader illustrations and one separate collection card were generated for this page. They are devotional interpretations, not historical images. The crocodile scenes are intentionally non-graphic.</p>
